{"name":"Sesame Crunch","hex":"#c26a35","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"sesame","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A tropical Asian plant (Sesamum indicum) bearing small flat seeds used as food and as a source of oil.","The seed of this plant."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/sesame"},{"term":"crunch","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To crush something, especially food, with a noisy crackling sound.","To be crushed with a noisy crackling sound."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A noisy crackling sound; the sound usually associated with crunching.\nCrunchiness.","Crunchiness."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/crunch"}],"entries":[{"title":"Sesame seed candy","blocks":[{"text":"Sesame seed candy is a confection of sesame seeds and sugar or honey pressed into a ball, bar or wafer. It is popular across East Africa, Middle East, South Asia, and East Asia, as well as in some European countries and Australia. The texture may vary from chewy to crisp. It may also be called sesame (seed) candy/bar/crunch; sesame seed cake may refer to the confection or to a leavened cake or cookie incorporating sesame."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7c/Pasteli_varieties.JPG/330px-Pasteli_varieties.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sesame_seed_candy","wikidata":"Q926500"},{"title":"Sesame","blocks":[{"text":"Sesame is a plant in the genus Sesamum, also called benne. Numerous wild relatives occur in Africa and a smaller number in India. It is widely naturalized in tropical regions around the world and is cultivated for its edible seeds, which grow in pods. World production in 2024 was 6.7 million tonnes, with India, Myanmar, and Sudan as the largest producers."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/70/Sesamum_indicum_-_K%C3%B6hler%E2%80%93s_Medizinal-Pflanzen-129.jpg/330px-Se